I am not very far into this game so I can't say much about the over all story but what I can say is this;
Owlboy is a "platformer" that will go down in history, I think. The animation is superb. The emotion that these 2d sprites can evoke is phenomenal. The general atmosphere is truly something to get lost in. While at first, I was getting some Fez vibes which, say what you will, can be a good or a bad thing, but very quickly I started to notice that that would not end up being the case. Characters have motive and purpose, and the setting is purposely set quite differently to the tone of the games characters. It's written much more like a good film from Pixar than it is like a typical platformer. I can't say yet whether it's worth the price tag but with the time and effort put into it, I can't really say there's any going wrong with buying this game. There is a lot of love behind it and it's easy to see. It's absolutely not for everyone but everyone should try it, nonetheless. Maybe it'll make a convert out of you.
I'll update the review as I get further (probably)

For a game that is still on Alpha, it successfully manages to hook players in for a long period of time despite its early content and diversity.
A game that despite its current early stage is already fully capable of projecting an enchanting medieval atmosphere throughout the entire game play either on its singleplayer or in real time multiplayer battles.
Kingdom Wars 2: Battles can be seen as a huge improvement from similar game series such as The Settlers, that is also capable of defining with accuracy the true way of RTS games genre.
Charming and wonderful graphics that makes players feel an unique medieval atmosphere in it;
A game that shows a lot of diversity so far despite its short variety available (which is understandable considering that this game is still on Alpha);
Both game mechanics as well the user interface are simple and good looking;
A crafting system that makes this game different from the usual RTS games genre;
Good music selection that contributes to the medieval atmosphere this game has to offer visually;
And last but not least, the first impression that I obtained after playing this game is that it has a bright and promising future ahead of it!
Lack of players;
The game currently has bugs and lack of content that can give your opponent serious advantage over you, but such things will eventually be fixed through patches;
Unstable multiplayer mode with usual de-syncs and some lag spikes from time to time.
My conclusion on this game is a bit hard to determinate because it is far from finished, but one thing I can guarantee, that this game has potential and a promising future ahead of it!

Pretty much your standard adventure/puzzle/hidden object game, except not quite. On normal, it seems pretty eager to let you skip puzzles/hidden object searches after a short time, and quite actively leads you around the adventure parts by highlighting relevant places to go/things to click.
Downsides:
The usual adventure problems where the solution can be a bit obtuse, but nothing too taxing;
Hidden object is so naturally evil (unexpected interpretations of the words, physically improbable hiding places), but this doesn't go too far into that kind of thing;
Some of the puzzles are a bit boring.
Upsides:
Time travel plot;
Hilariously dodgy character models;
Good variety of locations

